{"name":"Pickled Ginger","hex":"#ffdd55","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pickled","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of pickle"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Preserved by pickling.","Drunk."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pickled"},{"term":"ginger","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The pungent aromatic rhizome of a tropical Asian herb, Zingiber officinale, used as a spice and as a stimulant and acarminative.","The plant that produces this rhizome."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a reddish-brown color.","Having hair or fur of this color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ginger"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gari (ginger)","blocks":[{"text":"Gari (ガリ) is a type of tsukemono. It is made from sweet, thinly sliced ginger that has been marinated in a solution of sugar and vinegar. Younger ginger is generally preferred for gari because of its tender flesh and natural sweetness. Gari is often served and eaten after sushi, and is sometimes called sushi ginger. It may also simply be called pickled ginger. In Japanese cuisine, it is considered to be essential in the presentation of sushi. Some believe it is used to cleanse the palate between eating different pieces of sushi, or, alternatively, it may be eaten before or after the meal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Eingelegter_Ingwer_%2825966203794%29.jpg/330px-Eingelegter_Ingwer_%2825966203794%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gari_(ginger)","wikidata":"Q1494260"},{"title":"Beni shōga","blocks":[{"text":"Beni shōga (紅生姜) is a type of tsukemono. It is made from thin strips of ginger pickled in umezu (梅酢), the vinegary pickling solution used to make umeboshi. The red color is traditionally derived from red perilla. Commercial beni shōga often derives its hue from artificial coloring. It is served with many Japanese dishes, including gyūdon, okonomiyaki, and yakisoba."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Gyudon_by_jetalone_in_Higashi-Ginza%2C_Tokyo.jpg/330px-Gyudon_by_jetalone_in_Higashi-Ginza%2C_Tokyo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beni_sh%C5%8Dga","wikidata":"Q817282"},{"title":"Pickling","blocks":[{"text":"Pickling is the process of preserving or extending the shelf life of food by either anaerobic fermentation in brine or immersion in vinegar. The pickling procedure typically affects the food's texture and flavor. The resulting food is called a pickle, or, if named, the name is prefaced with the word \"pickled\". Foods that are pickled include vegetables, fruits, mushrooms, meats, fish, dairy and eggs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Gherkins_and_Onions.JPG/330px-Gherkins_and_Onions.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pickling","wikidata":"Q1503967"},{"title":"Ginger","blocks":[{"text":"Ginger is a flowering plant whose rhizome, ginger root or ginger, is widely used as a spice and a folk medicine. It is an herbaceous perennial that grows annual pseudostems about one meter tall, bearing narrow leaf blades. The inflorescences bear flowers having pale yellow petals with purple edges, and arise directly from the rhizome on separate shoots."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Koeh-146-no_text.jpg/330px-Koeh-146-no_text.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginger","wikidata":"Q35625"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Tursucu_Sahne_sokak_beyoglu_Istanbul_0644.jpg/500px-Tursucu_Sahne_sokak_beyoglu_Istanbul_0644.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x